  "servers": [
    {
      "description": "Production server",
      "url": "https://api.custify.com"
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    {
      "description": "People are the individual users within your application. They usually have a name and attributes like \u201cemail address,\u201d \u201cphone,\u201d etc.\n\nYou can create, update, and delete people and add or update custom attributes describing them. Customers can be viewed individually or as a list.\n\n### Limitations\n- Any integer values in the customer model are limited to the int32 range (-2^31 to 2^31-1).\n- Fields of the type String are limited to 255 characters.\n\n### Custom attributes\nThe custom_attributes object allows you to send any information you wish about a customer with the following restrictions:\n- Field names must not contain periods (`.`) or dollar (`$`) characters.\n- Field names must be no longer than 190 characters.\n- Field names must not contain only numbers.\n- Field values must be JSON Strings, Numbers or Booleans; Objects and Arrays will be rejected.\n- String field values must be no longer than 255 characters.\n- Maximum of 250 fields.\n- Custom attribute as a Date: send it in one of the Date formats described earlier. If the name of your custom attribute ends with \"_at,\" we'll automatically treat it as a Date rather than a String.\n\n### Unsetting attributes\nThe custom attributes and some of the default attributes (`name`, `phone`, `signed_up_at`, `session_count`, `unsubscribed_from_emails`, `unsubscribed_from_calls`) can be unset from a people. An example of request could look like:\n```json\n{\n  \"user_id\": \"123\",\n  \"$unset\": [\"plan\", \"custom_attributes.int_team_mates\"]\n}\n```\nThe people can be identified using the fields `id` (Custify internal ID), `user_id` (your internal ID of the people) or the `email`. The field `$unset` should be an array of strings representing the field names you want to unset using the dot(`.`) notation.\nIn the same request you can set and/or unset fields. Also you cannot set and unset the same field in a request.\n",
      "name": "People",
      "x-displayName": "PEOPLE"
    },
    {
      "description": "Events are generated by your users (\u201cpeople\u201d within Custify) while they use your application. You can also send \u201ccompany-only\u201d events, which are not related to a particular person. Events can be things like \u201cprinted an invoice,\u201d or \u201clogged into the platform,\u201d and represent an activity that a user is performing within your application.\nYou can track as many events as you wish, although we recommend focusing on the relevant ones for your business. Events are an essential part of the logic within Custify, and only if you track all relevant events will you be able\nto create proper segments, monitor customer lifecycles, and automate your customer success workflows properly. If there's an event for your user and the user is part of one or more companies you can link the event also to a specific company.\n\n### Limitations\n- Any integer values in the event model are limited to the int32 range (-2^31 to 2^31 -1).\n- Fields of the type String are limited to 100 characters.\n- Each event can contain up to `50` metadata key values.\n- Metadata object is limited to 10,000 characters.\n",
      "name": "Event",
      "x-displayName": "EVENTS"
    },
    {
      "description": "Companies represent commercial entities using your product, and they bundle people together within one organization. Companies typically have names like \u201cACME Ltd\u201d or \u201cBig Cooperation ABC,\u201d and have attributes like \u201cplan,\u201d \u201cmonthly revenue,\u201d \u201cwebsite\u201d etc. You can create and update companies and add custom attributes describing them.\nBesides the pre-defined (mostly optional) attributes, Custify offers you the option to add any kind of custom attributes to companies. These will all be visible within the application later on. Examples would be \"generated revenue,\" \"payment plan,\" \"address,\" or \"primary contact.\"\nThe \u201cchurned\u201d attribute is a special attribute used to indicate that this customer left you and can later be used within Custify to build segments and learn specific behaviors of such customers. Although optional, it can be a good addition to your set of data.\n\n### Limitations\n- Any integer values in the company model are limited to the int32 range (-2^31 to 2^31-1).\n- Fields of the type String are limited to 255 characters.\n\n### Custom attributes\nThe custom_attributes object allows you to send any information you wish about a company with the following restrictions:\n- Field names must not contain periods (`.`) or dollar (`$`) characters.\n- Field names must be no longer than 190 characters.\n- Field names must not contain only numbers.\n- Field values must be JSON Strings, Numbers or Booleans; Objects and Arrays will be rejected.\n- String field values must be no longer than 255 characters.\n- Maximum of 250 fields.\n- Custom attribute as a Date: send it in date one of the Date formats described earlier. If the name of your custom attribute ends with \"_at\", we'll automatically treat it as a Date rather than a string.\n\n### Unsetting attributes\nThe custom attributes and some of the default attributes (`name`, `plan`, `signed_up_at`, `owners_csm`, `owners_account`, `size`, `website`, `industry`, `monthly_revenue`) can be unset from a company. An example of request could look like:\n```json\n{\n  \"company_id\": \"123\",\n  \"$unset\": [\"plan\", \"custom_attributes.int_team_mates\"]\n}\n```\nThe companies can be identified using the fields `id` (Custify internal ID) or `company_id` (your internal ID of the company). The field `$unset` should be an array of strings representing the field names you want to unset using the dot(`.`) notation.\nIn the same request you can set and/or unset fields. Also you cannot set and unset the same field in a request.\n",
      "name": "Company",
      "x-displayName": "COMPANIES"
    },
    {
      "description": "Notes allow you to annotate and comment on your people or companies.\n",
      "name": "Note",
      "x-displayName": "NOTES"
    },
    {
      "description": "A tag allow you to label your people.\n",
      "name": "Tag",
      "x-displayName": "TAGS"
    },
    {
      "description": "Custom data objects stored on Companies or People\n",
      "name": "CustomData",
